  "hints": [
    "Money needs transactions and constraints. Which database family gives you that?",
    "Concurrent charges on the same account must be serialized. Clients also retry, so writes need to be idempotent.",
    "API Gateway (2) \u2192 Payments Service (2) \u2192 Redis Lock (2) and Postgres (3 replicas: writes on the primary, balance reads on replicas)."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 50,
    "costBudget": 1000
  },
  "explanation": [
    "A ledger is append-only: every payment writes balanced debit and credit entries in one SQL transaction, and the balance is derived from them.",
    "Idempotency keys make retries safe: the API stores the key with the result, and a repeated request returns the stored result instead of charging again.",
    "Per-account locks (or SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE) stop two concurrent charges from both passing a balance check.",
    "The API gateway handles authentication and per-merchant rate limits before requests reach the payments service."
  ]
}
